WEBVTT

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.010
In one controlled trial,

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:08.440
experienced developers used AI tools on their

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:09.640
own mature projects,

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:12.210
and came out almost a fifth slower.

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:13.300
The striking part:

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:14.920
they believed they had been a fifth

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:15.640
faster.

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:17.780
Your gut is not a measuring instrument.

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:19.200
And on a team,

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:20.690
that matters more than anywhere.

00:00:21.456 --> 00:00:23.776
The ten x claim isn't wrong,

00:00:23.776 --> 00:00:24.876
just misleading.

00:00:24.876 --> 00:00:26.016
It's a spike graph,

00:00:26.016 --> 00:00:28.056
not a flat multiplier.

00:00:28.056 --> 00:00:30.336
Boilerplate and big refactors go from hours

00:00:30.336 --> 00:00:31.276
to minutes.

00:00:31.276 --> 00:00:33.656
System design and a subtle race condition

00:00:33.656 --> 00:00:34.736
barely move.

00:00:34.736 --> 00:00:36.366
And agents amplify.

00:00:36.616 --> 00:00:38.476
Teams with good tests and small batches

00:00:38.476 --> 00:00:39.326
get better.

00:00:39.476 --> 00:00:41.196
Weak foundations get worse.

00:00:41.196 --> 00:00:42.356
So measure outcomes,

00:00:42.356 --> 00:00:44.196
like lead time and how often you

00:00:44.196 --> 00:00:44.956
ship.

00:00:44.956 --> 00:00:46.506
Not lines generated.

00:00:47.296 --> 00:00:49.116
Then the pull requests pile up.

00:00:49.116 --> 00:00:51.016
An engineer who opened two a day

00:00:51.016 --> 00:00:52.316
now opens five,

00:00:52.316 --> 00:00:53.256
all clean,

00:00:53.256 --> 00:00:54.556
all passing tests,

00:00:54.556 --> 00:00:56.686
and reviewing them is exhausting.

00:00:56.696 --> 00:00:58.496
So the question shifts from,

00:00:58.496 --> 00:00:59.756
is this correct,

00:00:59.756 --> 00:01:00.376
to,

00:01:00.376 --> 00:01:01.966
is this the right approach?

00:01:02.236 --> 00:01:03.926
Reviewers become architects.

00:01:04.156 --> 00:01:06.036
A review bot can take the first

00:01:06.036 --> 00:01:06.656
pass,

00:01:06.656 --> 00:01:07.696
but it's a filter,

00:01:07.696 --> 00:01:08.716
not a reviewer.

00:01:08.716 --> 00:01:10.116
It can find a bug.

00:01:10.116 --> 00:01:11.716
It can't tell you the feature shouldn't

00:01:11.716 --> 00:01:12.566
exist.

00:01:13.137 --> 00:01:14.957
The hardest question is juniors.

00:01:14.957 --> 00:01:16.837
They learned by doing exactly the work

00:01:16.837 --> 00:01:17.937
agents now do.

00:01:17.937 --> 00:01:19.787
The struggle was the education.

00:01:19.877 --> 00:01:22.007
So use the agent as a tutor,

00:01:22.017 --> 00:01:23.047
not a replacement.

00:01:23.377 --> 00:01:24.647
First it explains.

00:01:24.697 --> 00:01:25.767
Then it pairs,

00:01:25.797 --> 00:01:27.937
and every line must be understood before

00:01:27.937 --> 00:01:28.777
it's kept.

00:01:28.777 --> 00:01:30.027
Then it drafts.

00:01:30.137 --> 00:01:32.007
Only then does it multiply.

00:01:32.901 --> 00:01:35.251
Uneven adoption creates a new risk.

00:01:35.381 --> 00:01:36.701
One engineer gets fluent,

00:01:36.701 --> 00:01:38.381
ships it three times the rate,

00:01:38.381 --> 00:01:40.121
and soon has touched every part of

00:01:40.121 --> 00:01:41.001
the codebase.

00:01:41.001 --> 00:01:42.961
The bus factor drops to one.

00:01:42.961 --> 00:01:44.471
The fix is structural,

00:01:44.521 --> 00:01:45.561
a shared agents,

00:01:45.561 --> 00:01:46.361
.md,

00:01:46.361 --> 00:01:47.821
shared skills in the repo,

00:01:47.821 --> 00:01:48.731
and rotation,

00:01:48.741 --> 00:01:50.621
so the team's knowledge keeps up with

00:01:50.621 --> 00:01:51.471
its output.

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:53.650
Even the stand -up changes.

00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:55.720
A task can now start and finish

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:57.140
between two stand -ups.

00:01:57.140 --> 00:01:59.040
So the daily sync is less about

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:01.110
progress and more about intent.

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:03.740
Making sure three engineers aren't sending agents

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:05.210
after the same problem.

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:07.960
And conventions matter more because they shape

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:09.750
every agent session on the team.

00:02:10.499 --> 00:02:12.759
When agent written code causes an incident,

00:02:12.759 --> 00:02:13.789
who is responsible?

00:02:13.879 --> 00:02:16.009
The answer is simpler than it sounds.

00:02:16.139 --> 00:02:17.489
Git is the audit trail:

00:02:17.779 --> 00:02:19.679
mark every agent assisted commit,

00:02:19.679 --> 00:02:20.529
consistently.

00:02:20.559 --> 00:02:22.739
The reviewer who approves the pull request

00:02:22.739 --> 00:02:23.299
owns it,

00:02:23.299 --> 00:02:24.689
like any other code.

00:02:24.759 --> 00:02:26.119
And keep the session logs,

00:02:26.119 --> 00:02:27.779
so months later you can see what

00:02:27.779 --> 00:02:28.709
the agent saw.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:30.760
So what do you hire for?

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:32.180
Not whiteboard speed.

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:33.390
System design,

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:34.140
judgment,

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:35.130
clear communication,

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:37.120
and breaking big work into agent sized

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:37.970
pieces.

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:39.370
Hire for judgment.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:40.860
You can always give them an agent

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:41.790
for the rest.

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:42.820
Next up:

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:43.720
final words,

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:45.510
and why the craft is changing,

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:46.430
not dying.
